        "verified": false,
        "avatar": "https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/elJmfq8RQBh-ACMX9yWMrQb9nVxwvUsv1R3LmPqIDhllI0T7Trf8nVpokjO7JcXcIRMTRaS8Jp2an3pZx_UAKqLtgWzVV02dASEKw45xBDlZ7qDgxHOtBIIiUnazP1dt7YsEt8kMwY8O6VjJN5uS7FlGLMwFkRgt7AJe80ga4vw7exWLpuyJ2g9Kxi9X_9gH1B2E0CtZ0YTSGPLsw7F7Ol_hVv_UnyrgA2ugBAyPEBk0G3ZgPMZdr2HPwpoRZ9nBrCpE6jVv-MABdchXZK6xRnIV13Dpf-zIEwcs6zUGMmmTAu5a-PXo5_8i1lRA0ghOPdxsOFcV8WK4-uUAjMcsog.jpg"
      },
      "sourceId": "eg:medmannews",
      "sourceCountryCode": "eg",
      "contentType": "text",
      "media": [],
      "hasMediaInOriginal": false,
      "fallbackReason": null,
      "text": "Since Med is gone, it falls to me to continue his inane faggotry which he calls his talks. I want to talk about the conformist side of the average human.\n\nNaturally, we as human beings, are social animals. From Adam and Eve to now. When we were still fighting to find ourselves on top of the food chain, groups of humans formed to hunt with each other, since it helped them get more food, and to be safe. Unlike other animals, it was harder to rouse from our slumber in case of a threat, and thus trusted other humans to take the night watch to look out for the ones asleep. Humans did not climb to the top of the food chain alone. They had to work together. Now naturally within these groups, there had to be some sort of boundary on what behaviour was acceptable and what wasn't in order to stay a part of the group. People had to conform to these rules if they wanted to survive and stay a part of the group, which, truly, had them better off. \n\nTimes changed. We went from hunting and living together, to farming together, forming societies, to building cities, and then, forging empires. Over time, the definitions for what was socially acceptable behaviour became more clear and for the most part, it was the same all over the world. Don't insult the gods, don't murder, don't steal, which you can argue are just laws for humans to follow and you would be right. But then come the unspoken rules, such as you must dress like everyone else in your social class, you must remember your station, you must work as your father worked, you must be subservient to authority regardless of whether they are just or not; to name a few. \n\nNowadays, marching into the industrial age to what we are today, it changed again. In fact, you could say that these unspoken rules change almost everyday, and you're expected to know what those are. We as a species have never been so interconnected today, so it's there. These days it's something like you must be willing to go through a normal curriculum in school and get taught what everyone gets taught, you must eye a high paying job in the world and climb the corporate ladder, you must respect people, even those that do not respect you, you must go and obey whatever authority says regardless of how unjust it is or how your own personal feeling about it is, or be branded an outcast.\n\nThe more things change, the more they stay the same, especially when it comes to the expectation that you must follow these unspoken rules or risk censure or isolations. Coming into the turn of the millennium, when all these LGBT advocates came along, you could argue that it was some sort of rebellion, but their mandate eventually became normalized and what they talked about they got, for the most part. Now whoever talks against this same mandate, is now themselves, rebelling. One has to ask, however. When what you want changed is accepted and changed, does it still count as non conformist if what was seen as non conformist becomes such a big part of society? \n\nI would argue no. If those up there suddenly find themselves singing your tune, there is a reason for that that benefits them, as well. Which means they just needed an excuse to change things. \n\nBut then Bourbon, what is that one thing that is truly conformist, you ask.\n\nI would say that remaining true to yourself, keeping your core self the same, and holding belief and value in things regardless of what is accepted and what isn't accepted is true rebellion. What is accepted by society, like I said before, changes almost everyday, and so do people, to align themselves with the tides. But to stand against the tide like a rock, to believe in what you believe, unless you have good reason to believe otherwise, to change yourself for what you believe is better and not what society determines what is better. That way you keep yourself honest in a dishonest world. And one must stay true to it, regardless of what others say, and don't say.\n\nYou may be ostracized for it. You may be isolated by others for it. But you will never look back with regret.",
